WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.050
For years in the western world.

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:12.130
It was thought to be blasphemous to build
higher than the a church spire.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:16.230
The stories of the old testament warned against
reaching too close to the heavens.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:21.150
The builders of the tower of Babel declared:
“Come, let us build us a city and a tower

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.000
with its top in the heavens.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.050
And let us make a name for ourselves, lest
we be scattered upon the face of the whole

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:29.970
earth”
But god punished them for building a monument

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:35.630
to humanity and not to God by confusing their
language so they could no longer work together.

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:39.960
This story is used as the source of our world’s
languages for the religious and some still

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:44.780
use it as an argument against our modern world,
but it has interesting undertones for the

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:46.670
development of our cities.

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:51.210
For centuries the church spire remained as
the focal point of most western cities.

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:56.200
The Trinity Church was the largest building
in New York until 1890 when the New York World

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:57.850
Building was completed.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:02.750
This marked an end to the Cathedral dominated
skylines of many cities across the world.

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:06.720
Those church spires served as a symbol of
piety, but The New York World Building’s

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.750
height allowed Joseph Pulitzer to expand his
growing business without having to find a

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.880
large swath of land on the outskirts of the
city.

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:17.900
Growing taller served a practical purpose
and it still does in many cases.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:23.110
It’s that demand for space that truly drives
up the average height of buildings in cities.

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:27.330
Cities like Hong Kong do not have any supertall
buildings, but the average height of buildings

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:32.000
in Hong Kong is among the highest in the world
and that is largely driven by the lack of

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:33.530
space available.

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:37.540
Hong Kong is confined by the sea on one side
and the Chinese border on the other while

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:39.220
having a very mountainous landscape.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:42.130
There isn’t a huge amount of land to build
on.

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:45.890
This forced buildings to grow taller to accommodate
the city's population.

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:46.890
.

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:50.490
When Hong Kong needed to expand their International
Airport there was little space available.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:54.600
Instead they decided to level two islands
outside the city to create a new artificial

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.440
Island, where the new airport is now located.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:01.390
This project added 1% to the total surface
area of Hong Kong.

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:05.470
When space is limited humans are forced to
get inventive to cope, but in many cities

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:10.239
across the world space is not an issue and
these cities usually decide to expand outwards.

00:02:10.239 --> 00:02:13.950
This is called urban sprawl and it’s been
a topic of debate lately, with calls to stop

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:16.430
this decentralisation of cities.

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:20.620
Urban sprawl requires little micro-management
of resources, you simply continue to expand

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:24.750
current utilities and roads and approve buildings
on cheaper undeveloped land.

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:29.400
It’s an easy solution to a growing population,
but it creates many problems of it’s own

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.620
and is completely unsustainable as populations
grow.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:36.920
You cannot simply keep expanding the city
and allowing those problems to escalate.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:40.500
It has huge environmental and social impacts.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:44.860
One of the most obvious, which my friend Wendover
Productions spoke about in his last video,

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:46.500
was an increasing commute time.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:50.850
With an increasing city diameter the distances
we have to cover to reach the city centre

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:55.920
increases and it is incredibly difficult to
serve all of these far flung suburban neighbourhoods

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:57.850
with adequate public transport.

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:02.160
This results in a city dependant on the car,
our least efficient form of transport.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:05.810
This not only has a social impact, as long
commute times are one of highest and most

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:10.990
controllable factors that affect our happiness,
but The average American spends 17,600 minutes

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:15.420
behind the wheel a year, much of that is spent
in gridlock traffic, that’s equivalent to

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:21.930
spending almost an extra 37 days at your traditional
8 hour 9 to 5 job, but it also has a direct

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:25.040
impact on pollution and air quality in the
city too.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:29.060
That’s 17,600 minutes of a car polluting
the environment.

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:34.140
Reducing a city's dependence on cars reduces
our carbon footprint on this world.

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:36.880
Urban sprawl affects our environment in other
ways too.

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.690
Spreading our cities creates water distribution
problems.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:43.920
Here in Ireland it is thought that up to 50%
of the treated water is lost through pipe

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.950
leakage and that problem is not unique to
Ireland.

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:52.650
In 2010 it was reported that 3.3 billion litres
of water was being wasted in the England and

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:54.600
Wales through pipe leakage.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:58.390
Reducing the sprawl reduces the length of
pipes needed and thus reduces the chances

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:03.280
of leakages and the problem can be attenuated
further by creating buildings with self sustaining

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:04.840
water supplies.

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:08.480
This is becoming a growing trend and consideration
among engineers.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:13.260
LEED or Leadership in Energy and Environmental
Design is one of the most popular green building

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:15.740
certifications used world wide.

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:19.880
It rates how resource efficient buildings
are in their construction, energy use and

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:20.910
water use.

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:25.630
The Taipei 101 was awarded LEED’s highest
certification with a platinum certificate.

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:29.970
It achieved this with it’s own dedicated
water management systems and low-flow water

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:30.970
fixtures.

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:35.419
This design ideology helped the Taipei 101
to decrease it’s potable water consumption

00:04:35.419 --> 00:04:40.980
by at least 30% compared to the average building
consumption, saving about 28 million liters

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:43.370
of potable water annually.

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:47.940
When you also consider that in America landscape
irrigation is estimated to account for nearly

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:54.410
one third of all residential water use, totaling
nearly 9 billion gallons of water per day.

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:58.730
It would be vastly more sustainable for a
world where water is in ever increasing demand

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:04.440
to create cities where we have a larger percentage
of LEED certified high rise residential buildings.

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:08.949
This goes beyond just environmental impacts,
there are a number of socio-economic motivations

00:05:08.949 --> 00:05:11.030
to creating more high rise buildings too.

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:15.310
I pointed out that the urban sprawl results
in longer commutes and the roads and public

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:18.940
transportation needed to facilitate those
commutes are not free.

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:23.139
They need to be maintained and built with
your tax money and while building up is more

00:05:23.139 --> 00:05:27.690
costly, that cost and risk is usually incurred
on private contractors and the costs of building

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:30.950
up starts to decline once you reach a certain
height.

00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:35.410
To build a skyscraper there are a number fixed
costs, but many of these costs do not increase

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:37.070
with the height of the building.

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:41.470
Fixed costs like the cost of land, legal fees
and design costs can be offset by building

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:42.470
higher.

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:46.860
If the building is built on a 100 square metre
plot of land and the building has 40 stories,

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:50.120
each floor only takes up 2.5 square metres
of land.

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:54.810
That has obvious economic advantages, especially
when you consider design and material costs

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:58.910
only start to go up when you reach around
the 40th floor and that critical height is

00:05:58.910 --> 00:06:01.699
likely to go up as technology improves.

00:06:01.699 --> 00:06:05.600
Buildings like the Burj Khalifa may be just
exuberant displays of wealth, but they do

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:08.550
serve as technology demonstrators.

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:13.070
For 25 years the tallest building in the world
was the Sears Tower, now known as the Willis

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:14.070
Tower.

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:18.470
It uses a bundled tube structure, which maximises
the amount floor space, but if we scale this

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:20.290
building to the size of the Burj Khalifa.

00:06:20.290 --> 00:06:25.690
It’s floor space would be dominated by structural
elements and the interior would have no natural

00:06:25.690 --> 00:06:26.690
light.

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:30.590
As mentioned in my last video, the buttressed
core of the Burj Khalifa provides the structural

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:36.260
integrity needed to reach these heights, while
maximizing both the window access and usable

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:37.260
space.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:41.050
This is vital knowledge and experience to
have to allow building heights to keep growing

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:43.220
while keeping costs down.

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:46.460
So you may be thinking why aren’t there
more high rises buildings.

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:49.860
If there are all these benefits there must
be reasons that we aren’t building more

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:50.860
of them.

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:52.810
We will learn why after this quick side note

