00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:45.170
So they primary reason we aren’t building
higher is because of city planning and regulatory

00:07:45.170 --> 00:07:46.170
problems.

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:50.090
Take New York’s growth in the early 20th
century as an example, building heights were

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:52.370
growing and many were unhappy with it.

00:07:52.370 --> 00:07:56.820
At the time 5th Avenue was filled with stately
mansions, homes to the wealthy families of

00:07:56.820 --> 00:07:59.410
New York like the Carnegies and Rockefellers.

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:03.120
They worried that unless building heights
were restricted, 5th avenue would turn into

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:07.090
a dark cannon, overshadowed by these towering
behemoths.

00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:11.729
These worries led to 1916 zoning resolution
which allowed buildings to grow in height,

00:08:11.729 --> 00:08:13.889
but restricted their width as they grew.

00:08:13.889 --> 00:08:18.039
This is one of the primary reasons so many
buildings in New York built during that era

00:08:18.039 --> 00:08:19.600
taper towards their top.

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:23.670
It was a measure to prevent buildings from
blocking the sunlight below, but the regulation

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:26.870
had loop holes and architects quickly exploited
them.

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:32.149
Between 1916 and 1960 the city’s zoning
code was amended 2500 times.

00:08:32.149 --> 00:08:37.920
The 1961 Zoning Resolution brought in strict
rules and introduced a new floor to area ratio

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:42.479
rule that restricted buildings heights according
to the district they were.

00:08:42.479 --> 00:08:46.600
The floor to area ratio set how much floor
space could be built on a plot of land.

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:51.560
A floor to area ratio of 2 means you can build
a 2 story building on your full plot or a

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:53.569
4 story building on half your plot.

00:08:53.569 --> 00:08:59.250
R1, R2 and R3 districts are low density zones
like Staten Island and the Jamaica Estates

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:03.689
in Queens and they have a floor to area ratio
of 0.5.

00:09:03.689 --> 00:09:08.319
Where as major thoroughfares in Manhattan
are R9 and R10 districts which have floor

00:09:08.319 --> 00:09:12.050
to area ratios of 7.5 and 10 respectively.

00:09:12.050 --> 00:09:15.970
This floor to area ratio rule put pressure
on designers to allocate more space to open

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:22.079
plazas or other public spaces around the building
to faciltate a taller tower, whereas the 1916

00:09:22.079 --> 00:09:25.860
zoning laws resulted in tiered buildings that
started right on the sidewalk.

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:30.970
The 1961 zoning code encouraged privately
owned public space to ease the density and

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:36.240
claustrophobia of a high rise city and I think
we can all agree that is a move in the right

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:37.240
direction.

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:40.500
Zoning regulations like this are important
to prevent brainless growth that destroy a

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:45.089
city’s character, but sometimes they are
overzealous and prevent modernisation altogether.

00:09:45.089 --> 00:09:50.209
Take Washington DCs zoning code that has been
in place for over 100 years with little change.

00:09:50.209 --> 00:09:55.420
The Height of Buildings act of 1910 prevents
any building beyond 40 metres in height.

00:09:55.420 --> 00:09:59.920
That is incredibly restrictive and it has
resulted in a city where the tallest structure

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:03.120
is a giant stone obelisk and this thing.

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:07.999
Even with a relatively small population, Washington
has some of the worst traffic in the US.

00:10:07.999 --> 00:10:12.220
A study released this year by INRIX found
that the people of Washington waste an average

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:15.040
of 75 hours per year in traffic.

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:19.569
That means their journeys take 75 hours more
than if there was no congestion.

00:10:19.569 --> 00:10:24.220
They were second only to Los Angeles, who
waste an average of 81 hours a year in traffic.

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:29.230
LA is often singled out as the key example
of this problem of unchecked outward growth.

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:33.160
It was a key theme in the movie “Her”,
where the car dominated urban sprawl of the

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:37.949
present is juxtaposed with a vision of a glossy,
clean, high rise future for LA.

00:10:37.949 --> 00:10:42.009
The main character Theodore lives in a highly
developed downtown LA.

00:10:42.009 --> 00:10:44.689
He lives in a high rise building and works
in a high rise building.

00:10:44.689 --> 00:10:48.939
He’s able to walk between them and cars
seem to have ceased to exist,he instead uses

00:10:48.939 --> 00:10:51.389
the extensive metro system to get around.

00:10:51.389 --> 00:10:54.699
The movie even designed a futuristic subway
map of LA.

00:10:54.699 --> 00:10:59.679
To create this vision of the future, the producers
digitally enhanced the cities existing skyline.

00:10:59.679 --> 00:11:04.170
While also mixing in shots from present day
LA with numerous shots from Shanghai’s Pudong

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:07.949
district, like this pedestrian sky bridge
which allowed Spike Jonze to film Theodore

00:11:07.949 --> 00:11:11.730
wandering through the urban jungle without
having the cars at street level interfering

00:11:11.730 --> 00:11:15.860
with the illusion of a city that has transcended
the need for personal transport.

00:11:15.860 --> 00:11:20.019
That transformative change seems implausible
and not likely in the near future, but cities

00:11:20.019 --> 00:11:24.449
can undergo metamorphosis when money and regulations
are not an issue.

00:11:24.449 --> 00:11:28.149
Take the mid-19th century renovation of Paris
as an example.

00:11:28.149 --> 00:11:32.889
Paris was once described by one of it’s
residents as “an immense workshop of putrefaction,

00:11:32.889 --> 00:11:37.730
where misery, pestilence and sickness work
in concert, where sunlight and air rarely

00:11:37.730 --> 00:11:38.730
penetrate.

00:11:38.730 --> 00:11:43.370
A terrible place where plants shrivel and
perish, and where, of seven small infants,

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:45.899
four die during the course of the year.”

00:11:45.899 --> 00:11:50.559
This is an incredibly stark description of
Paris, when present day Paris is often fawned

00:11:50.559 --> 00:11:56.309
over for it’s wide boulevards, amazing architecture
and extensive public transport system.

00:11:56.309 --> 00:12:00.480
Paris of old was plagued with problems caused
by the outdated planning of it’s medieval

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:01.480
past.

00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:05.620
Paris was in need of renovation and Napolean
III made it possible by giving the money and

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:07.560
power needed to Baron Haussmann.

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:11.759
He transformed these narrow streets and old
dilapidated buildings into spacious boulevards.

00:12:11.759 --> 00:12:12.759
(Rue de Rivoli)

00:12:12.759 --> 00:12:16.619
He revamped all of these streets in red and
created two new parks for the cities residents.Napolean

00:12:16.619 --> 00:12:22.269
III and Haussmann helped transform Paris into
the charming city of light that 16 million

00:12:22.269 --> 00:12:24.769
tourists now visit every year.

00:12:24.769 --> 00:12:28.480
But it may be time to start rethinking Paris’
city planning once again.

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:32.679
The lack of housing in central Paris has caused
prices to raise so high that only the rich

00:12:32.679 --> 00:12:33.959
can afford it.

00:12:33.959 --> 00:12:37.799
Forcing the working class families of Paris
to the outskirts of the city.

00:12:37.799 --> 00:12:41.410
Creating huge disparity of wealth between
the centre and outskirts.

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:46.250
This map shows the concentration of social
housing as a percentage of total residences,

00:12:46.250 --> 00:12:50.370
with the largest percentages being located
furthest from the city centre and even now

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:55.589
these people are being forced further outside
the city limits as gentrification occurs.

00:12:55.589 --> 00:13:01.319
Paris is no stranger to revolts of the working
class with notable riots in 1968, 2006 and

00:13:01.319 --> 00:13:05.670
just this year Paris saw more riots as new
labour laws were passed giving employers more

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:09.959
power to increase working hours, decrease
holidays and decrease pay.

00:13:09.959 --> 00:13:14.319
The lack of affordable housing compounds these
social problems and the main cause of these

00:13:14.319 --> 00:13:18.989
prices is Parisians unwillingness to build
over existing buildings.

00:13:18.989 --> 00:13:22.829
During Haussmann's renovation of paris height
restrictions on buildings were raised from

00:13:22.829 --> 00:13:28.160
16.5 to 19 metres, but the transformation
of Paris took place in a time where elevators

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:29.529
did not exist.

00:13:29.529 --> 00:13:34.589
In 1967 the height restrictions were lifted
and the Montparnasse Tower was constructed

00:13:34.589 --> 00:13:35.589
soon after.

00:13:35.589 --> 00:13:37.970
A building that is loathed by Parisians.

00:13:37.970 --> 00:13:41.199
It sticks out from the surrounded buildings
like a sour thumb.

00:13:41.199 --> 00:13:44.379
There is a fine line between progress and
regression.

00:13:44.379 --> 00:13:48.639
Paris renovated to rid itself of the claustrophobic
narrow streets of the past, building higher

00:13:48.639 --> 00:13:51.529
without thought will bring it right back to
that.

00:13:51.529 --> 00:13:55.430
The construction of this building resulted
in the height restriction being reduced to

00:13:55.430 --> 00:13:56.999
25 metres for central Paris.

00:13:56.999 --> 00:14:01.660
France is a heavily regulated country and
when it’s rulers decide they don’t want

00:14:01.660 --> 00:14:03.269
change, change will not occur.

00:14:03.269 --> 00:14:08.130
But one part of Paris proves that modern high
rise buildings can be introduced without destroying

00:14:08.130 --> 00:14:09.489
the character of the city.

00:14:09.489 --> 00:14:15.529
La Défense is Europe’s largest purpose
built business district housing 180,000 daily

00:14:15.529 --> 00:14:16.529
workers.

00:14:16.529 --> 00:14:20.339
La Défense proves that skyscrapers can be
incorporated into the historic background

00:14:20.339 --> 00:14:25.379
of Paris without destroying it’s charm,
but La Défense is a financial district.

00:14:25.379 --> 00:14:30.220
It was built to create office space and houses
just 25,000 permanent residents.

00:14:30.220 --> 00:14:34.779
There is little motivation to build high rise
buildings to reduce housing prices as it is

00:14:34.779 --> 00:14:38.060
cheaper to push people to the outskirts of
the city.

00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:41.889
Paris is not alone in these problems, London
has been criticised for the same problems

00:14:41.889 --> 00:14:46.160
and Vice News made an excellent documentary
about the effects of this gentrification of

00:14:46.160 --> 00:14:48.019
working class neighbourhood.

00:14:48.019 --> 00:14:52.730
There is no easy way of balancing preservation
and growth, but we need to put our countries

00:14:52.730 --> 00:14:57.440
leaders under more pressure to consider this
and not just follow the cheap easy route,

00:14:57.440 --> 00:15:01.049
because the problems will only get worse as
our populations grow.

00:15:01.049 --> 00:15:05.029
If we allowed those height restrictions to
stop us from building on 5th Avenue, the world

00:15:05.029 --> 00:15:09.920
would have been deprived of iconic buildings
like the Empire State and Flat Iron Buildings.

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:14.639
Great cities are not static, they constantly
change and move with the times.

00:15:14.639 --> 00:15:18.399
The greatest of our modern cities like New
York and Singapore function because their

00:15:18.399 --> 00:15:23.339
height enables a huge number of people to
work and live on a small piece of land.

00:15:23.339 --> 00:15:30.339
That is something our world is going to need
going forward as our populations continue

00:15:30.339 --> 00:15:32.839
to grow.

00:15:32.839 --> 00:15:33.929
Thanks for watch.

